Excerpts from "Prior Restraint and Censorship" by Benno C. Schmidt,
Jr. in _The First Amendment: Selections from the Encyclopedia of the
American Constitution_ edited by Leonard Levy, et al., 1990 (a bargain
at $14):

"History has rooted in our constitutional tradition of freedom of
expression the strongest aversion to official censorship. We have
learned from the English rejection of press licensing and from our own
experiences that the psychology of censors tends to drive them to
excess, that censors have a stake in finding things to suppress, and
that -- in system of wholesale review before publication -- doubt
tends produce suppression. [...]

American law has tolerated requirements of prior official approval of
expression in several important areas [...]. No one may broadcast
without a license [...]. Licensing is also grudgingly tolerated --
because of the desirability of giving notice and of avoiding conflicts
or other disruptions of the normal functions of public places -- in
the regulation of parades, demonstrations, leafleting, and other
expression activities in public places. But the courts have taken
pains to eliminate administrative discretion that would allow
officials to censor public forum expression because they do not
approve of its message.

[...]

These decisions and others have firmly established that the First
Amendment tolerates virtually no prior restraints. This doctrine is
one of the central principles of our law of freedom of the press. On
the surface, the doctrine concerns only the form of controls on
expression. It bars controls prior to publication, even if imposition
of criminal or civil liability following publication would be
constitutional. [...] [It] is presumably an absolute bar to any
wholesale system of administrative licensing or censorship of the
press. [It] removes most of the opportunities for official control of
those types of expression for which general rules of control are
difficult to formulate. The message of the prior restraint doctrine is
that if you cannot control expression pursuant to general legislative
standards, you cannot control it at all -- or nearly at all, as the
_Pentagon Papers_ decision suggests, by suggesting an exception
allowing an injunction in a truly compelling case of national
security.

[...]

[W]here the law must wait to move against expression after it has been
published, time is on the side of freedom. All in all, even such prior
restraints as judicial injunctions -- which are more discrimination
than wholesale censorship -- tend toward irresponsible administration
and an exaggerated assessment of the dangers of free expression.

